# Line endings are decided here, not by whoever happens to be cloning.
#
# The AMS sync runs from two places: safe-pull-from-ams.ps1 on a Windows
# workstation, and the GitHub Action on ubuntu. With this left to each machine's
# core.autocrlf, the same file can be committed with different endings from the
# two, and git then sees every line of it as changed — a whole-file conflict in
# a file nobody edited. Pinning eol=lf makes the merge see content only.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.ttf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.jar  binary
*.keystore binary
*.lottie binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Merge policy for the AMS sync.
#
# `ams-update` is a vendor branch holding AMS's client tree and nothing else, so
# a sync merge presents AMS's copy of these files as "theirs". For the files
# below that copy is not an alternative worth considering — it describes the web
# deployment, which is not what this repo builds — and taking it, or stopping to
# ask about it, is pure noise. Drivers are registered by build/gitMergeSetup.mjs
# (run from npm's `prepare`, so a plain `npm install` is enough) and by the sync
# workflow.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The native project. AMS carries an android/ folder because Capacitor was
# scaffolded there once; ours is the one that ships, with the signing config,
# the FCM keys, the deep-link intent filters and the launcher icons.
android/**            merge=mobile

# App id, asset-server hostname, updater and plugin config: all of it describes
# this app, none of it has a meaningful web counterpart.
capacitor.config.json merge=mobile

# Ours documents the app and the sync; AMS's documents the web client.
README.md             merge=mobile

# Never merged line-by-line — a lockfile conflict is unresolvable by hand and
# meaningless when it happens. Ours is kept and then regenerated from the merged
# package.json, which is the only thing that actually decides its contents.
package-lock.json     merge=mobile

# Unioned rather than chosen. AMS adding a dependency has to reach the app —
# that is the whole point of the sync — while our Capacitor dependencies, our
# version (the OTA channel reads it) and our deploy:ota script have to survive
# it. See build/mergePackageJson.mjs.
package.json          merge=package-json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both sides appended here.
#
# The rest of the policy above is about files with an owner. These two entries
# are about the opposite case: files we and AMS genuinely co-own, where the
# conflicts have never been disagreements at all — both sides added something
# new at the same place, and the resolution was always "keep both".
#
# That has cost six OTA deploys: LoginForm (09-04, now an override), main.jsx
# twice (09-07, 09-08), GpuMetrics (09-10), .gitignore (09-12) and
# LearningLayout (09-12). Every one was a position collision in a region where
# position means nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A list of paths, one per line, in no meaningful order — the textbook case for
# git's builtin union driver. Both sides' lines are kept with no markers. The
# worst a wrong union can do here is ignore a path twice, or keep ignoring one
# that upstream stopped ignoring; neither is worth a failed deploy.
.gitignore            merge=union
.prettierignore       merge=union
.eslintignore         merge=union

# Sources are NOT unioned — union on real code silently produces nonsense. This
# driver resolves two shapes and nothing else: both sides purely inserting, where
# every inserted line is an import; and both sides editing the member list inside
# one import, where the answer is every member either side has. Anything else it
# hands back to git with ordinary markers. See the safety argument in
# build/mergeAdditive.mjs.
*.js                  merge=additive
*.jsx                 merge=additive
*.mjs                 merge=additive
*.cjs                 merge=additive
*.ts                  merge=additive
*.tsx                 merge=additive

# ...except under android/, which is ours whole. The globs above are broader
# than the `android/** merge=mobile` rule near the top of this file and a LATER
# attribute wins, so without this restatement a .js file in the native project
# (a Gradle script, a generated plugin list) would quietly start merging with
# AMS's scaffolded copy. Keep this last.
android/**            merge=mobile
